#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093446 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find all of the solutions to the equation\n\n![](images/03/039f2eaa5fc29449de1795a64aeb80391087cbbad093f0b1b8e3533bb024687f.svg)\n\nTo calculate a solution, we can reduce the equation to a more convenient form by dividing through by $\\cos x,$ and then use the identity $\\tan x = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}$ to simplify. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\sqrt{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\tan x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\tan x = \\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nWe can immediately calculate the principal value $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx = \\arctan(\\sqrt{3}) = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is $x_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$\n\nTo find the other solution in the given interval, we add $\\pi\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the solutions are $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}, \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e6a96a57-0e08-54b9-9ccf-a0facd4b8c41" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3925 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093447 :tutorial/content "Let's find all the solutions to the equation\n![](images/cd/cd3dc7a1fd4373b4448b79bf449892ef5eb7056fa762edc25aef73539f427ecc.svg)\n\nNotice that this time, the domain is $-\\pi <  x \\leq \\pi.$\n\nFirst, we divide each term of the equation by $\\cos x$ and use the identity $\\tan x = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}$ to simplify. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-1 & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\tan x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\tan x = 1\n$$\n\nWe can immediately calculate the principal value $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx =  \\arctan(1) = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is $x_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$\n\nTo find the other solution in the given interval, we *subtract* $\\pi.$ Hence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}-\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the solutions are $x = -\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4} , \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} .$" :tutorial/id #uuid "20a00eec-d00f-52e6-8e26-03eb611ec5fc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3926 :tutorial/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Using the Sin-Cos-Tan Identity Under a Modified Domain"}]}
